### What is the Foreign Memory API's Arena?

The Foreign Memory API, finalized in Java 22, provides `Arena` as a scope that owns off-heap `MemorySegment` allocations. When you close the arena, every segment it owns is freed immediately. This is deterministic lifetime management, meaning you control exactly when native memory is released.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.foreign.Arena;
import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment;
import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout;

public class PriceBufferService {

    public void loadTickPrices() {
        // Arena frees all segments when try-with-resources closes it
        try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
            MemorySegment prices = arena.allocate(
                ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 1024
            );
            // Write a tick price at index 0
            prices.setAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0, 150075L);
            long firstPrice = prices.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0);
            System.out.println("Tick price: " + firstPrice + " paise");
        }
        // Memory is freed here, no GC dependency
    }
}
```

### Why does DirectByteBuffer have a lifetime problem?

`ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(size)` allocates native bytes and returns a small on-heap wrapper. There is no public method to free the native bytes directly. They are released only when the wrapper object is garbage-collected and its `Cleaner` runs. That makes the native memory's lifetime coupled to the heap GC schedule, which is non-deterministic.

In a tight allocation loop you can exhaust native memory and get `OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory` even though the wrappers are collectible, because the collector has not run yet.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

public class DirectBufferProblem {

    public void demonstrateExhaustion() {
        // This loop can throw OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory
        // because GC has not reclaimed previous wrappers
        for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
            ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(1024 * 1024);
            buf.putLong(0, 150075L); // Write a price
            // buf goes out of scope, but native bytes linger until GC
        }
    }
}
```

### How does Arena provide spatial and temporal safety?

A `MemorySegment` knows its size, so every access is spatially bounds-checked. Reading past the end throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`. After the arena closes, segments are temporally invalidated, and any subsequent access throws `IllegalStateException` rather than silently reading freed or reused memory.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.foreign.Arena;
import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment;
import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout;

public class ArenaSafetyDemo {

    public void showSafetyChecks() {
        MemorySegment segment;
        try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
            segment = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 4);
            segment.setAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0, 50000L);

            // Spatial check: reading past the end
            try {
                long bad = segment.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 10);
            } catch (IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
                System.out.println("Spatial check: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        // Temporal check: segment is dead after arena close
        try {
            long stale = segment.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0);
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Temporal check: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Spatial check: Out of bound access on segment ...
Temporal check: Already closed
```

### What are the different Arena kinds?

The API provides four arena kinds for different use cases.

| Arena Kind | Thread Safety | Lifetime | Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Arena.ofConfined()` | Single-thread only | Deterministic on close | Hot-path allocations in one thread |
| `Arena.ofShared()` | Multi-thread safe | Deterministic on close | Shared buffers across threads |
| `Arena.ofAuto()` | Multi-thread safe | GC-dependent | When you do not want to manage lifetime |
| `Arena.global()` | Multi-thread safe | Never freed | Program-lifetime constants |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.foreign.Arena;
import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment;
import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout;

public class ArenaKindsDemo {

    // Shared arena for multi-threaded price feed
    public void sharedArenaExample() {
        try (Arena shared = Arena.ofShared()) {
            MemorySegment prices = shared.allocate(
                ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 256
            );
            // Multiple threads can read and write safely
            Thread reader = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> {
                long price = prices.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0);
                System.out.println("Reader got: " + price);
            });
        }
        // All segments freed deterministically here
    }
}
```

### How is Arena different from DirectByteBuffer? (Comparison)

| Feature | Arena + MemorySegment | DirectByteBuffer |
|---|---|---|
| Deallocation | Deterministic on close | GC-dependent via Cleaner |
| Spatial bounds | Checked on every access | Checked within capacity |
| Temporal safety | IllegalStateException after close | No explicit invalidation |
| File unmap | Deterministic on arena close | Non-deterministic |
| Native interop | First-class with FFM API | Not supported |
| Thread control | Confined or shared modes | No thread ownership |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.foreign.Arena;
import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment;
import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

public class ArenaVsDirectBuffer {

    // Old way: DirectByteBuffer, GC-dependent
    public void oldWay() {
        ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(8);
        buf.putLong(0, 250000L); // 2500.00 rupees in paise
        long price = buf.getLong(0);
        // No way to free immediately; waits for GC
    }

    // New way: Arena, deterministic
    public void newWay() {
        try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
            MemorySegment seg = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG);
            seg.set(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0, 250000L);
            long price = seg.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0);
        }
        // Memory freed right here
    }
}
```

### How does Arena integrate with file mapping?

Mapping files through an Arena gives deterministic unmap on arena close. A `MappedByteBuffer` has the same non-deterministic unmap problem as a direct buffer.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.foreign.Arena;
import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment;
import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.StandardOpenOption;

public class MappedFileService {

    public void readTransactionFile(Path path) throws Exception {
        try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined();
             FileChannel channel = FileChannel.open(
                 path, StandardOpenOption.READ)) {
            MemorySegment mapped = channel.map(
                FileChannel.MapMode.READ_ONLY, 0,
                channel.size(), arena
            );
            long firstAmount = mapped.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0);
            System.out.println("First transaction: " + firstAmount + " paise");
        }
        // File unmapped deterministically here
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with Arena?

The most common mistake is accessing a segment after its arena has closed. Another is using a confined arena from a different thread.

```java
// WRONG: accessing segment after arena close
MemorySegment seg;
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
    seg = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG);
    seg.set(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0, 100000L);
}
// seg.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0); // IllegalStateException!

// WRONG: accessing confined arena from another thread
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
    MemorySegment seg2 = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG);
    Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> {
        // seg2.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0); // WrongThreadException!
    }).join();
}

// CORRECT: use shared arena for multi-thread access
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofShared()) {
    MemorySegment seg3 = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG);
    seg3.set(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0, 100000L);
    Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> {
        long val = seg3.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, 0); // OK
        System.out.println("Shared read: " + val);
    }).join();
}
```

### When should you use Arena vs DirectByteBuffer?

Prefer Arena for new off-heap code where you want predictable release, large or long-lived off-heap structures, native interop via the FFM API, or memory-mapped files you want to unmap on demand. Keep DirectByteBuffer for interop with APIs that still require a `ByteBuffer`, such as NIO channel I/O, and for existing stable code that works well with the current model.

### What about thread safety and concurrency?

A confined arena restricts all segment access to the thread that created it. A shared arena allows any thread to access its segments, with the close operation coordinated so it cannot race with in-flight accesses. This means you cannot free memory out from under another thread, which prevents the classic use-after-free race condition.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Direct byte buffers remain fine for straightforward NIO channel I/O and much existing code. But for production off-heap services, the arena model is clearly better because it makes lifetime explicit and access safe. The modern guidance is to prefer the Foreign Memory API with arenas for new off-heap code. Pooling is still useful on hot paths, but with arenas you can confidently allocate and free in a scoped way without worrying about GC timing.
